                          "Markup": [
                            {
                              "Start": 66,
                              "Length": 5,
                              "URL": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/water",
                              "Type": "PubChem Internal Link",
                              "Extra": "CID-962"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "String": "* Worsening of electrolyte abnormalities due to the shift of free water into the intravascular space. These abnormalities may include hyponatremia, hypokalemia, and hypocalcemia.",
                          "Markup": [
                            {
                              "Start": 66,
                              "Length": 5,
                              "URL": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/water",
                              "Type": "PubChem Internal Link",
                              "Extra": "CID-962"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "String": "* Precipitation of crystals at low temperatures can cause vascular and end-organ damage."
                        },
                        {
                          "String": "Mannitol crosses the vessel wall, albeit poorly, and more easily crosses injured vessels, such as in patients with intracranial hemorrhage. Frequent doses of mannitol can result in the drug crossing the blood-brain barrier and exacerbating cerebral edema by drawing water into (instead of out of) the brain. This is particularly true for children with cerebral hyperemia.",
